<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll">
  <p>Start Network is a global humanitarian organisation whose members respond to crises across the world. When they wanted to improve their member community platform, they needed to understand how a hugely diverse global membership actually communicates, collaborates and shares knowledge, spanning local NGOs in Bangladesh and Pakistan through to international organisations in the UK and Philippines.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="sn-what">
  <h2 id="sn-what">What I did</h2>
  <p>I led mixed-methods discovery research combining in-depth semi-structured interviews with seven members across five countries and a survey distributed to the broader membership. Recruitment was designed to capture genuine regional diversity, including organisations in the Global South where connectivity, device access and communication norms differ significantly from those in the UK.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="sn-found">
  <h2 id="sn-found">What we found</h2>
  <p>The existing member portal was largely invisible. Most members either didn't know it existed or had delegated it to a colleague. But the real problem was deeper than awareness: the way members actually communicate, and what they need to do their jobs in a crisis, had never been properly mapped.</p>
  <p>Regional differences shaped everything. UK-based members kept work and personal devices separate, refused to use WhatsApp for professional purposes and preferred desktop tools. Members in Bangladesh, Pakistan and the Philippines relied heavily on mobile phones, used messaging apps interchangeably across devices and needed solutions that worked in low-connectivity environments or offline entirely.</p>
  <blockquote><p>I would not opt into a WhatsApp group... I don't have a work phone... it would mean that our private phones would just constantly blow up with messages.</p></blockquote>
  <p>Meanwhile, the Skype channels used exclusively for Start Network conversations had become unmanageable. Hundreds of messages a day made it impossible to extract anything critical.</p>
  <blockquote><p>There's a training going on... you get 140 messages and you're like "is there a massive crisis going on?"... no, it's just an update in the Skype channel.</p></blockquote>
  <p>What members actually needed wasn't a new communication tool. They needed a centralised knowledge hub where past learnings, case studies and crisis resources could be found quickly, especially when something urgent was unfolding.</p>
  <blockquote><p>What's happening outside the Philippines and what we are learning from other countries is something missing.</p></blockquote>
  <p>I recommended building a dynamic, AI-powered knowledge repository that could consolidate fragmented information streams, support real-time alerts and work across low-bandwidth environments. A membership directory was also recommended to enable direct connections without going through Start Network HQ.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study__section reveal-on-scroll" aria-labelledby="sn-changed">
  <h2 id="sn-changed">What changed</h2>
  <p>Start Network launched a new member community platform the following year. The selection and development process was directly shaped by this research.</p>
</section>
